Schema formulation:
```lean
theorem circleGrowthT (T : ℝ) (hT : 0 < T) (hT1 : 1 ≤ T) (hT2 : T ≤ 2) :
    ∃ G : UniformMeasureGrowth ({toGHSpace (AddCircle T)} : Set GHSpace),
      G.C = 2 ∧ G.K = 4 ∧ G.R = 1 ∧ (∀ s, 0 < s → G.m s = (min s (1/2)).toNNReal)
```
Hmm, an existential is weaker/awkward. Better: a *def* `circleGrowthT (T) [Fact (0<T)] (hT1) (hT2) : UniformMeasureGrowth {toGHSpace (AddCircle T)}` with the constants fixed. Then two corollaries `circleGrowthT_one` and `circleGrowthT_two` and their end-to-end conclusions. Plus a theorem that all these inhabitants share the same constants:

```lean
theorem circleGrowthT_constants (T) ... : (circleGrowthT T ...).C = 2 ∧ ... := ⟨rfl, rfl, rfl, rfl⟩
```

And a *uniform* statement: for every T ∈ [1,2], the family `{toGHSpace (AddCircle T)}` is compact and has a pointed subsequence with certificate:
```lean
theorem isCompact_circleT (T) ... : IsCompact ({toGHSpace (AddCircle T)} : Set GHSpace)
theorem exists_pointed_subseq_circleT ...
```

This demonstrates: the same explicit constants work for every circumference in [1,2], and each singleton family satisfies the whole chain. It's a decent addition.

